WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:19.680
1066 CE.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.370
The English Throne sits vacant after the King
dies without an heir.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:29.320
Three claimants to the title quickly rise,
and prepare to do battle over who would wear

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.410
the crown.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:35.190
At the end of this turbulent year, invasions
would be mounted, battles would be fought,

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:41.420
and only one man would be left standing with
the prize grasped in his bloody fist.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:47.649
It is a tale that reads easier as fiction
than fact, and in fact has been the inspiration

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:52.290
for many famous fictional dynastic struggles,
including Game of Thrones.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:58.190
This is the story of the victor of this struggle,
who conquered a kingdom and changed the fate

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.620
of a country and a continent.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:06.259
This is the story of William the Conqueror.

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:09.789
Early Life

00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:12.579
Our story begins around 1000 CE.

00:01:12.579 --> 00:01:16.280
Viking raiders have been terrorizing the coasts
of Europe for over 200 years.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.119
They took vast tracts of land by conquest,
including the north coast of France by the

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:25.259
English Channel, which eventually became known
as the Duchy of Normandy.

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:29.599
Further advances came on the British Isles,
most notably in 1013, when the King of Denmark

00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:34.880
conquered England, and Danes ruled the country
for 30 years before being driven out by the

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.429
native Anglo-Saxons.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:40.689
In these early feudal times, the central government,
controlled by the King, was weak and decentralized,

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:48.819
the land broken up into a series of earldoms,
duchies, and baronies, ruled by an Earl, Duke,

00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:49.819
or Baron.

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:54.619
These provincial leaders were, in theory,
vassals of the King, and sworn to him by an

00:01:54.619 --> 00:01:59.289
oath of fealty, but in practice, they were
very much autonomous, and struggles between

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:03.359
the King and the provinces, and between the
provinces themselves, were common.

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:09.079
William was born around 1028 CE, the illegitimate
son of Robert I, Duke of Normandy.

00:02:09.079 --> 00:02:13.489
This led to his original nickname, usually
used by his enemies: William the Bastard.

00:02:13.489 --> 00:02:18.069
His father seemed to have little interest
in marrying any of his mistresses, which meant

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:21.969
that William was considered the heir to the
duchy from a very young age.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:27.640
In 1035, Robert embarked on a pilgrimage to
Jerusalem, against the advice of his advisors.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:32.280
He made it to the holy city, but fell ill
and died on the return journey, leaving William,

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.170
at age 7, the Duke of Normandy.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:40.290
As was typical when a child inherited a province
or kingdom, there was great instability in

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:45.189
Normandy as the region's nobles and William’s
relatives fought each other for control of

00:02:45.189 --> 00:02:46.500
the region and of the young Duke.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.959
A series of William’s guardians were killed,
including one, Osbern, in the Duke’s bedchamber

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:52.049
while he slept.

00:02:52.049 --> 00:02:58.200
Things came to a head in 1047, when Guy of
Burgundy rose in rebellion against the ducal

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.010
government, supported by other nobles.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.459
They attempted to capture Duke William at
Valognes, had they succeeded, he almost certainly

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:05.709
would have been killed.

00:03:05.709 --> 00:03:10.719
But William escaped and fled south to Paris,
seeking refuge with King Henry I. William

00:03:10.719 --> 00:03:15.110
soon returned, however, at the head of an
army, and together with the forces of King

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.819
Henry, they defeated the rebels at the Battle
of Val-es-Dunes.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:25.519
At age 18, Duke William was already showing
himself to be a capable soldier and commander.

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:30.819
The rebellion of 1047 was not to be the end
of Normandy’s internal strife.

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:34.409
William continued to deal with rebellious
nobles until 1054.

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:38.870
At this point, alarmed by the consolidation
of power under William, King Henry turned

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:42.109
against his vassal and led an army into Normandy.

00:03:42.109 --> 00:03:46.859
But he was soundly defeated by William’s
forces and chased out of the duchy.

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:50.719
He tried again in 1057, and was defeated again.

00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:56.969
This enmity continued until King Henry’s
death in 1060.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:00.680
English Concerns

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.999
By 1060, William had largely taken control
of Normandy and put down internal opposition

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:05.999
to him.

00:04:05.999 --> 00:04:11.000
With the death of King Henry, his 7 year old
son Phillip was named king, but was too young

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.000
to rule.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.250
One of the regents appointed to rule the kingdom
during Phillip’s minority was William’s

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:21.319
father in law, Baldwin V, Count of Flanders,
so there would be no more trouble from the

00:04:21.319 --> 00:04:22.970
crown for the time being.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.460
So William turned his focus towards expansion.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.060
The Duke acquired some territory on the Continent,
such as the County of Maine, and fought a

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:38.009
campaign in Brittany, but his keenest interest
lay to the north, across the Channel, to England.

00:04:38.009 --> 00:04:42.410
It was becoming increasingly apparent to everyone
that King Edward the Confessor would die without

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:47.870
producing an heir to his throne, leaving a
power vacuum that various factions would rush

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:48.870
to fill.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:54.039
The most logical internal candidate to succeed
Edward was Harold Godwinson, the Earl of Essex.

00:04:54.039 --> 00:04:58.620
Harold was a leading member of the Godwin
family, the most powerful nobles in the kingdom.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.629
He was also Edward’s brother in law.

00:05:00.629 --> 00:05:03.880
However, Duke William also felt he had a legitimate
claim to the throne.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:09.070
At one point, in 1051, the Godwins fell out
of favor with King Edward and were exiled.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:13.660
It’s believed that the King reached out
to William at this point and named him his

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.810
heir: Edward had lived in Normandy for 30
years after his father was deposed by the

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:23.530
Danes, protected by the Dukes of Normandy,
so the connection made sense.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:28.430
However, a year later, the Godwins returned
to England and reconciled with Edward, thus

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.849
leaving the question of the succession extremely
murky.

00:05:32.849 --> 00:05:38.160
Some historical sources tell us that Harold
Godwinson traveled to Normandy around 1064,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.690
and met with Duke William.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:44.160
It’s said that he swore an oath during that
meeting to uphold William’s claim to the

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.180
throne of England.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.950
However, the only sources that document this
are Norman, and there’s no way to know if

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:54.740
this actually took place or was Norman propaganda.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:00.220
The Game Begins

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:02.669
King Edward the Confessor died January 5th,
1066.

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:06.860
On his deathbed, he named Harold Godwinson
as his heir.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.860
Harold acted quickly, crowning himself king
the next day and quickly gaining the support

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.300
of the English nobility.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.500
Across the Channel in Normandy, Duke William
was furious.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.669
He knew at this point that the only way to
uphold his claim to the throne would be to

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:20.979
take it by force.

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:24.110
He vowed to raise an army and invade England.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:28.659
This ambition was hardly secret, and all of
Europe watched and waited throughout the spring

00:06:28.659 --> 00:06:32.970
and summer of 1066 as William prepared his
invasion force.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:37.349
Harold, of course, heard of William’s plan,
and raised an army of his own to counter the

00:06:37.349 --> 00:06:39.030
threat of a Norman invasion.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:43.780
He spent most of the summer in the south of
England waiting for Duke William to make his

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:44.780
move.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:49.419
However, most of Harold’s force was composed
of civilian militia levees that needed to

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:53.480
return to their fields to harvest their crops,
and the king was forced to dismiss a large

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.189
part of his army on September 8th.

00:06:55.189 --> 00:06:59.610
And to make matters worse, around the same
time, Harold learned that another claimant

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:55.990
to the throne had appeared.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:01.190
The Norsemen Attack

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:32.470
A year before the events of 1066, Harold Godwinson’s
brother Tostig had been stripped of his earldom

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.220
of Northumbria and driven into exile.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.791
Infuriated by what he saw as his brother’s
betrayal, he went to the King of Norway, Harald

00:08:39.791 --> 00:08:45.020
Hardrada, and convinced him to make a claim
for the English throne and invade from the

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:46.260
north of the country.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:50.360
Harald believed he had a legitimate claim
to the throne of England because he was descended

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:55.210
from the line of Vikings that had conquered
and ruled England for thirty years before

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:56.760
Edward the Confessor took over.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:01.850
Harald also spent the summer of 1066 making
preparations for his invasion, and left Norway

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:07.000
at the end of August with a fleet of 300 ships
and an army of 15,000 men.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:11.540
He landed in England in early September, and
met up with Tostig.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:15.710
Together they attacked towns and villages
throughout Northumbria, and defeated an English

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.710
army led by the local lords.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:22.410
This prompted the surrender of the great city
of the north, York.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:27.340
In the south, Harold learned of the Norweigan
invasion, and quickly turned north with as

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.120
many soldiers as he could muster.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.410
He made the 185 mile trip north in only 4
days, riding day and night.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:40.220
On September 25th, 1066, the Norse army left
their encampment and marched to Stamford Bridge,

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:45.280
where they expected to meet with representatives
of the city of York to receive hostages and

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:46.280
supplies.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:50.630
Not expecting a fight, they left most of their
heavy armor back at the camp.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:56.430
Imagine their surprise then, when they arrived
to find not cowed townspeople, but the English

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:59.160
royal army waiting for them.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.870
Alarmed, Harald Hardrada and Tostig Godwinson
rode forward to meet their enemy.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.980
King Harold approached them, alone.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:10.420
He spoke to his brother, offering him his
earldom back if he turned against Hardrada

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:11.780
and sided with him.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:15.950
Tostig asked what Harold would offer Hardrada
for his trouble.

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:22.090
“Seven feet of English ground, for he is
taller than other men,” Harold replied coolly.

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:26.030
There could be no negotiating with rival claimants
to the throne of England.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:28.340
And Tostig refused to switch sides.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.590
The Battle of Stamford Bridge was on.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:35.540
The Norwegians began to retreat across the
bridge, a stronger position waiting for them

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:37.780
on the other side as the English attacked.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:42.880
Harold’s army was held up on the bridge
by a single Norse soldier, a berserker armed

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.870
with a two handed great axe.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:50.460
He killed 40 men single handedly before he
was finally killed by an English soldier who

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:53.500
floated under the bridge and stabbed him from
beneath.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.520
The English rushed across the bridge, meeting
the shield wall of the Norse.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:10:58.920
The battle was furious.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:03.880
At some point, Harald Hardrada was mortally
wounded by an arrow, and Tostig Godwinson

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.240
was slain on the field.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:09.370
The lack of heavy armor on the part of the
Norse made the difference, and their army

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:11.040
was cut to pieces.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.210
Once the English broke through the Norse shield
wall, the battle became a slaughter.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:18.200
By the time it was over, 10,000 were dead.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:20.750
Harold allowed the survivors to return to
Norway peacefully.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:26.880
So many had died that only 25 of the original
300 ships were needed to transport them back.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.210
Never again would a Scandinavian army threaten
England.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.880
The Age of the Vikings was over.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:35.480
But Harold didn’t have time to celebrate
his victory.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:39.320
Instead, he immediately turned around and
marched south again.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.490
Because he knew Duke William was coming.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:47.580
The Norman Invasion

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:52.270
Just three days after the Battle of Stamford
Bridge, on September 28th, the Norman army

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:55.260
landed at Pevensey Bay on the south coast
of England.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.640
Like Harold, William didn’t know of the
Norwegian invasion, but it worked out nicely

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:02.810
for him because it allowed him to land unopposed.

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:07.600
The Duke moved his forces to the nearby town
of Hastings, where he built a wooden castle.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:13.400
There he was content to wait, close to his
supply lines via the sea, for Harold to come

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:14.980
to him.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:18.820
Harold heard of William’s invasion while
he was marching south, but proceeded more

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:23.560
slowly than he had when responding to the
Norwegian invasion: his army was still recovering

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:28.150
from the Battle of Stamford Bridge, and he
had to leave a large part of it in the north.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:32.860
Even still, he probably moved TOO quickly,
had he waited a few more days, he likely would

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:36.160
have had more men at his disposal to face
the Normans.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:39.950
As it was, Harold’s army encamped at the
top of a steep slope on the night of October

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:44.150
13th, a few miles north of William’s fortified
camp at Hastings.

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:48.510
His efforts to surprise William failed, and
the Normans knew he was coming.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:54.270
The morning of October 14th dawned unusually
bright and warm for October in England.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:58.280
Duke William’s Norman army left its camp
and marched to where the English were.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:02.490
William hoped to settle the question of who
should rule England with a single decisive

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:03.490
battle.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:07.690
Whoever won was clearly ordained by God to
sit on the throne, he believed.

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:08.690
The Battle of Hastings

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:17.480
The 
English army was almost entirely composed

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:20.660
of infantry, with no cavalry and few if any
archers.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:25.480
With this in mind, Harold kept his forces
on top of the hill, having his front rank

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:30.500
lock shields into an impenetrable wall that
would fend off the Norman infantry and cavalry

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:33.000
attacks, and blunt the effect of the Norman
archers.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.910
And that’s exactly what happened during
the early stages of the battle.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:41.520
Repeated charges by the Norman cavalry were
repulsed, and the Norman infantry were unable

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.270
to break through the shield wall.

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:47.690
The French archers were hampered by the steep
slope, firing either directly into the shield

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:50.030
wall, or over the heads of the English.

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:54.390
Then, the first decisive moment of the day:
the left flank of the Norman army suddenly

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:57.000
broke and ran in a panicked flight.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:00.440
A rumor had spread through the ranks that
Duke William had been killed, and the Normans

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:04.190
wanted no part in continuing the battle without
their leader.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:08.770
Even though Harold ordered his army to remain
on the hill, some disobeyed and chased the

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:09.990
fleeing Normans.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:14.450
William was not dead, and he rode through
the ranks of the fleeing soldiers, removing

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:17.050
his helmet to show them that he was still
in the fight.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:21.000
He managed to turn them around and attack
the pursuing English.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.640
Outside the protection of the shield wall,
the English took heavy casualties.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:28.970
A lull settled over the battlefield as the
soldiers from both armies rested.

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:34.410
The retreat of his left flank had given William
a new idea: if he had portions of his army

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:40.070
pretend to retreat, he could provoke the English
to follow, and engage them off the hill and

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:41.720
without the shield wall.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:46.680
When the battle recommenced, it’s believed
he did this at least twice, with moderate

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:47.680
success.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.250
The English were taking casualties, but the
line held.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:54.500
The issue was still very much in doubt as
afternoon turned into evening.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.490
Soon darkness would make further fighting
difficult, if not impossible.

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:04.440
Just as it seemed nothing would be settled
that day after all, catastrophe suddenly struck

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:05.440
the English.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:09.030
King Harold was hit in the eye with an arrow,
going into his brain.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.120
He fell from his horse, killed instantly.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.880
Without their king, the English army was leaderless,
and quickly fell apart.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:20.530
The Normans pursued the fleeing English for
a while, but were stopped by darkness.

00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:25.700
It didn’t matter: the Battle of Hastings
was over, and there was only one claimant

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:29.920
to the English throne left standing.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:33.340
King William

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:37.831
Despite his victory and the death of Harold,
the English still resisted the Norman invasion

00:15:37.831 --> 00:15:42.000
for another two months, forcing William to
take the city of London by siege.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:47.241
But soon enough, it was over, and on Christmas
Day 1066, William was crowned as the King

00:15:47.241 --> 00:15:48.241
of England.

00:15:48.241 --> 00:15:52.150
It took another five years for William to
pacify his new realm, as he dealt with a series

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:54.860
of rebellions by the Anglo-Saxon aristocracy.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:58.830
William dealt with this by building a series
of castles throughout England, giving them

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:02.030
to his supporters to keep order over the land.

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:06.780
By the time William died, England was dotted
with over 500 castles.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:11.150
The most famous fortress that William built
was the White Tower in London, which eventually

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:14.450
became the central keep of the Tower of London.

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:18.990
One of William’s key problems was his inability
to be in two places at once: when he was in

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:21.120
England, there were uprisings in Normandy.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:24.320
And when he was in Normandy, there were rebellions
in England.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:28.360
William spent most of the first ten years
of his reign travelling between the two countries,

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:32.280
dealing with rebels, while also gradually
transforming English life.

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:37.240
Over time, most of the Anglo-Saxon nobles
had been replaced by Normans, and the ones

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:41.370
who were left began to marry into Norman and
other French families.

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:45.320
Official documents were written in Latin or
French instead of Old English.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:48.680
Two works enhance our modern understanding
of this period.

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:54.820
The first is the Bayeux Tapestry, an incredible
230 foot long piece of embroidered cloth commissioned

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:58.410
by William’s brother Odo shortly after the
Battle of Hastings.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:03.990
The tapestry depicts the events of 1066 from
the Norman side, from King Edward’s death

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:06.270
to the victory of the Normans at the Battle
of Hastings.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:12.730
Incredibly, this tapestry has survived intact
for over 900 years and can still be seen today

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:15.459
at a dedicated museum in Bayeux.

00:17:15.459 --> 00:17:20.569
The second work was commissioned by King William
in December 1085.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:25.009
He wanted a complete record of all the royal
holdings in England, a record that came to

00:17:25.009 --> 00:17:27.100
be called “The Domesday Book”.

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:31.460
This book documented all the lands of the
English realm in great detail, including who

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:36.250
had controlled the land before the Conquest,
how much tax revenue the area produced, the

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:41.070
number of people who lived there, even absurdly
specific details such as how many chickens

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:46.070
a particular farmer had, or how much milk
a dairy farm had produced in a given year.

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:50.590
This record, preserved in the National Archives
in London, offers us great insight on the

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:56.669
disposition and administration of England
during the time of the Norman conquest.

00:17:56.669 --> 00:17:59.899
Final Days

00:17:59.899 --> 00:18:06.740
In 1087, William returned to Normandy to deal
with his eldest son Robert, who was allied

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:10.549
with the King of France in an attempt to undermine
him.

00:18:10.549 --> 00:18:15.080
While engaged in this campaign, William was
injured by the pommel of his saddle, and the

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:16.419
wound grew infected.

00:18:16.419 --> 00:18:20.419
He was taken to Rouen where his condition
quickly deteriorated.

00:18:20.419 --> 00:18:22.360
William knew he needed to settle his affairs.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:27.750
He left Robert the duchy of Normandy and his
second son William was given control of England.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:31.789
Once it became clear that the king was going
to die, his advisors and courtiers all left

00:18:31.789 --> 00:18:33.970
him to settle their own affairs.

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:39.980
As a result, William died completely alone
on September 9th, 1087, aged 59.

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:43.950
In the immediate aftermath of the king’s
death, his sons Robert, William, and Henry

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.510
all fought each other over control of the
kingdom.

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:50.549
Normandy itself eventually became formally
a part of the Kingdom of France, and the heritage

00:18:50.549 --> 00:18:52.899
of the Vikings was swept away.

00:18:52.899 --> 00:18:56.880
The course of English history, meanwhile,
was changed significantly by the events of

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:58.220
the Norman conquest.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:03.759
Prior to the conquest, England had more closely
identified culturally with Scandinavia than

00:19:03.759 --> 00:19:05.139
with continental Europe.

00:19:05.139 --> 00:19:09.490
Afterwards, England was brought closer to
France, ties which bound it into the Middle

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:13.870
Ages and caused a centuries-long rivalry between
the two countries.

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:17.990
The story of the Norman conquest is one of
the most famous in world history.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:23.200
It was a momentous event that shaped the course
of European history for centuries to come.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:29.100
And it bestowed upon William I, King of England
and Duke of Normandy, a new title, one that

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:44.580
no one is likely to forget: William the Conqueror.

